WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:01.619
During his two terms as governor of

00:00:01.620 --> 00:00:03.449
Arizona, Doug Ducey did an awful

00:00:03.450 --> 00:00:05.159
lot. He managed to pass a flat

00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:07.208
income tax with a rate of 2.5%, he

00:00:07.209 --> 00:00:09.119
reformed public sector pensions,

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:10.829
universalized some of the most

00:00:10.830 --> 00:00:12.239
important school choice measures in

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:13.649
the state, reformed occupational

00:00:13.650 --> 00:00:15.389
licensing rules, and turned a budget

00:00:15.390 --> 00:00:16.709
deficit into a surplus.

00:00:16.710 --> 00:00:18.869
He substantially reduced the size

00:00:18.870 --> 00:00:20.219
of the state's government workforce

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:21.959
as well. He built a makeshift border

00:00:21.960 --> 00:00:23.609
wall though out of shipping crates

00:00:23.610 --> 00:00:24.869
and pushed back on marijuana

00:00:24.870 --> 00:00:25.739
legalization.

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:26.909
And he was accused by his

00:00:26.910 --> 00:00:29.099
constituents of doing both too much

00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:30.839
and too little during COVID.

00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:32.399
Today, he runs Citizens for Free

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:34.349
Enterprise. In December, he received

00:00:34.350 --> 00:00:35.879
Reason's Savas Award for

00:00:35.880 --> 00:00:37.259
Privatization, which is given

00:00:37.260 --> 00:00:38.909
annually to someone who is advancing

00:00:38.910 --> 00:00:40.409
innovative ways to improve the

00:00:40.410 --> 00:00:41.759
provision and quality of public

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:43.739
services by engaging the private

00:00:43.740 --> 00:00:45.389
sector. When he was in town, the

00:00:45.390 --> 00:00:46.889
governor and I sat down to talk

00:00:46.890 --> 00:00:48.179
about his time in office, his

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:49.229
worries about the future of the

00:00:49.230 --> 00:00:50.969
Republican Party, his commitment to

00:00:50.970 --> 00:00:52.589
fusionism, and why Arizona

00:00:52.590 --> 00:00:54.270
politicians are so weird.

00:00:59.340 --> 00:01:01.049
Thank you for joining us, Governor.

00:01:01.050 --> 00:01:01.859
We are excited to talk with you here

00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:02.699
at Reason today.

00:01:02.700 --> 00:01:03.719
Good to be here.

00:01:03.720 --> 00:01:05.068
Good. Thanks for having me.

00:01:05.069 --> 00:01:06.989
Let's start just right into

00:01:06.990 --> 00:01:09.149
it. Arizona politicians

00:01:09.150 --> 00:01:10.709
are kind of built different.

00:01:10.710 --> 00:01:12.569
What is it about Arizona that

00:01:12.570 --> 00:01:14.789
seems to just generate a little

00:01:14.790 --> 00:01:16.679
bit of a kind of heterodox

00:01:16.680 --> 00:01:18.209
or unorthodox politician, would you

00:01:18.210 --> 00:01:19.279
say?

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:20.819
Yeah, I don't know.

00:01:20.820 --> 00:01:22.139
I think it's a good question.

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:24.029
I think maybe the fact that we're

00:01:24.030 --> 00:01:26.189
the youngest state in the lower

00:01:26.190 --> 00:01:28.079
48, that we're

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:30.059
a place where so many people

00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:31.620
came to live.

00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:35.499
So few people that are there today

00:01:35.500 --> 00:01:37.239
were actually born there.

00:01:37.240 --> 00:01:39.099
So people make that decision.

00:01:39.100 --> 00:01:40.479
And then I think there's something

00:01:40.480 --> 00:01:42.309
about the West in the spirit of

00:01:42.310 --> 00:01:44.319
Barry Goldwater, where it brings

00:01:44.320 --> 00:01:46.359
an independent mindedness to

00:01:46.360 --> 00:01:46.959
it.

00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:48.789
Arizona has been red of

00:01:48.790 --> 00:01:50.889
late, but it's trending blue.

00:01:50.890 --> 00:01:52.869
What do you what do you attribute

00:01:52.870 --> 00:01:53.709
that to?

00:01:53.710 --> 00:01:55.119
Candidates matter.

00:01:55.120 --> 00:01:56.769
I would actually push back pretty

00:01:56.770 --> 00:01:58.629
hard on where

00:01:58.630 --> 00:02:00.579
it is. I was able

00:02:00.580 --> 00:02:02.409
to win in 2014 with

00:02:02.410 --> 00:02:04.389
the wind at my back and

00:02:04.390 --> 00:02:06.819
win by a larger margin

00:02:06.820 --> 00:02:09.069
in 2018

00:02:09.070 --> 00:02:10.478
with the wind in my face.

00:02:10.479 --> 00:02:12.669
What was it really a tough year

00:02:12.670 --> 00:02:14.589
for conservatives and

00:02:14.590 --> 00:02:16.839
Republicans around the country.

00:02:16.840 --> 00:02:19.059
And I was also able to capture

00:02:19.060 --> 00:02:20.919
44% of the

00:02:20.920 --> 00:02:22.779
Hispanic vote against an

00:02:22.780 --> 00:02:25.149
opponent named David Garcia.

00:02:25.150 --> 00:02:27.429
So if you have the right candidate

00:02:27.430 --> 00:02:29.229
who's talking about common sense

00:02:29.230 --> 00:02:31.179
kitchen table issues and

00:02:31.180 --> 00:02:33.699
actually persuading the electorate,

00:02:33.700 --> 00:02:35.379
I think the state is still

00:02:35.380 --> 00:02:37.059
a center-right state.

00:02:37.060 --> 00:02:38.229
If you have somebody that wants to

00:02:38.230 --> 00:02:40.839
come and relitigate 2020

00:02:40.840 --> 00:02:42.729
and only speak to the base, that's

00:02:42.730 --> 00:02:43.730
a losing message.

00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:46.539
You campaigned in your first

00:02:46.540 --> 00:02:48.249
campaign on bringing taxes in

00:02:48.250 --> 00:02:49.989
Arizona as close as possible to

00:02:49.990 --> 00:02:51.909
zero, and you

00:02:51.910 --> 00:02:54.069
got to 2.5%

00:02:54.070 --> 00:02:55.929
flat tax in the end.

00:02:55.930 --> 00:02:57.399
How did you do that?

00:02:57.400 --> 00:02:58.839
I feel like as long as I have been

00:02:58.840 --> 00:03:00.729
involved in kind

00:03:00.730 --> 00:03:02.769
of free market or small

00:03:02.770 --> 00:03:03.999
government politics, people have

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:05.889
been talking about low tax,

00:03:05.890 --> 00:03:07.089
flat tax. You're going to do your

00:03:07.090 --> 00:03:08.259
taxes on a postcard.

00:03:09.520 --> 00:03:11.049
Usually more talk than action.

00:03:11.050 --> 00:03:12.339
So what happened?

00:03:12.340 --> 00:03:13.959
Persistence, persistence,

00:03:13.960 --> 00:03:15.009
persistence.

00:03:15.010 --> 00:03:15.969
It was our goal.

00:03:15.970 --> 00:03:18.519
Every year we lowered or simplified

00:03:18.520 --> 00:03:20.679
taxes and we actually

00:03:20.680 --> 00:03:22.569
had the left overreach and

00:03:22.570 --> 00:03:25.239
came into Arizona and I think

00:03:25.240 --> 00:03:27.189
deceived the voters

00:03:27.190 --> 00:03:29.499
with an initiative saying

00:03:29.500 --> 00:03:31.059
we can put a billion dollars

00:03:31.060 --> 00:03:33.519
additional into K-12 education

00:03:33.520 --> 00:03:35.589
and it won't cost you any money.

00:03:35.590 --> 00:03:37.239
Only the rich people.

00:03:37.240 --> 00:03:40.179
And they took our 4.5%

00:03:40.180 --> 00:03:42.099
tax at the highest progressive

00:03:42.100 --> 00:03:43.959
level to eight.

00:03:43.960 --> 00:03:45.849
Now, 8% in Arizona would

00:03:45.850 --> 00:03:47.349
have been a cancer that would have

00:03:47.350 --> 00:03:49.209
metastasized over decades.

00:03:49.210 --> 00:03:50.227
That's Bernie Sanders' Vermont,

00:03:51.420 --> 00:03:53.469
Washington, D.C., or

00:03:53.470 --> 00:03:54.999
New York State.

00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:56.199
But it was popular.

00:03:56.200 --> 00:03:57.999
We worked hard to beat it.

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:01.179
It was polling at about 65-35.

00:04:01.180 --> 00:04:03.069
We were able to drive it down to

00:04:03.070 --> 00:04:04.929
51%

00:04:04.930 --> 00:04:06.579
on Election Day.

00:04:06.580 --> 00:04:08.229
But when I was a when I was a young

00:04:08.230 --> 00:04:09.849
boy, there was a show on Saturday

00:04:09.850 --> 00:04:12.429
morning, Wild World of Sports.

00:04:12.430 --> 00:04:13.839
And they would talk about the thrill

00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:15.939
of victory and the agony

00:04:15.940 --> 00:04:17.229
of defeat.

00:04:17.230 --> 00:04:19.208
We suffered about 18 months

00:04:19.209 --> 00:04:21.039
of the agony of defeat while

00:04:21.040 --> 00:04:23.649
we challenged the initiative in

00:04:23.650 --> 00:04:25.479
court. It eventually got to

00:04:25.480 --> 00:04:27.129
our Supreme Court, and then we

00:04:27.130 --> 00:04:28.989
reformed taxes in

00:04:28.990 --> 00:04:30.339
the legislature.

00:04:30.340 --> 00:04:32.469
In the interim, the law,

00:04:32.470 --> 00:04:34.359
the initiative was struck down

00:04:34.360 --> 00:04:36.669
and we had reduced taxes along

00:04:36.670 --> 00:04:38.529
the way. So had we

00:04:38.530 --> 00:04:40.929
been successful on Election Day,

00:04:40.930 --> 00:04:42.579
taxes in Arizona today would be

00:04:42.580 --> 00:04:44.229
4.5%.

00:04:44.230 --> 00:04:46.359
But because we had a bad result,

00:04:46.360 --> 00:04:48.669
we persisted

00:04:48.670 --> 00:04:50.499
in the legislature and we had a

00:04:50.500 --> 00:04:52.179
Supreme Court that was not going to

00:04:52.180 --> 00:04:54.249
let out-of-state interest deceive

00:04:54.250 --> 00:04:55.119
the voter.

00:04:55.120 --> 00:04:57.009
Today, we have the lowest flat tax

00:04:57.010 --> 00:04:58.059
in the nation.

00:04:58.060 --> 00:05:00.009
So I would say a combination of

00:05:00.010 --> 00:05:01.989
good planning, good

00:05:01.990 --> 00:05:03.399
timing,and good luck.

